# 3.5: the weak-bot leak and what is next

Where the next ranks are

Unit 3, talk 5

---

## The leak

At 20th, 3.4's performance index is:

- **+5.1** against the top 100
- **-1.7** against the 716 robots ranked below 500

That deficit costs about **1.0 APS**, twice the gap to 19th place. Lifting every negative weak-tier PBI to zero was worth 1.26 APS, about rank 13.

---

## Is it live-only? No

Hypothesis 0: if it were live-only stalls, the bench would not show it.

- The bench shows it. 3.4 scored 75.9% against vort.Chaser on the bench, 74.7% live.
- 3.4 lost **203 of 3,850** rounds to 11 weak bots
- Against rammers it spent most of every round within 100 px, where they fire power 3

So it is tactics, and tactics can be fixed.

---

## RAM-2: run from a rammer

- Once a robot has charged to within 120 px in **two different rounds**, run from it for the rest of the battle
- Each tick, drive the heading that keeps a pursuing robot furthest away over the next 20 ticks
- Fire full power
- Rammers gained +7.1 to +20.1 points. Rounds lost: 36 to 4.

---

## MIR-1: plan a path against a mirror

A mirror mover drives to the reflection of our position through the centre.

Offline, on the truth logs, aiming at that reflection hit very differently:

| Aim | MirrorNano | MirrorMicro |
|---|---|---|
| Head-on | 30% | 18% |
| Reflection of a straight-line guess | 15% | 9% |
| Reflection of our real path, 2 ticks late | **71%** | **86%** |

So MIR-1 plans our path 110 ticks ahead and follows it exactly.

---

## The weak-set result

| 11 weak bots, 10 battles each | 3.4 | 3.5 |
|---|---|---|
| Mean share | 74.8% | **86.8%** |
| Rounds lost of 3,850 | 203 | **69** |

MirrorNano +16.2, MirrorMicro +15.3. Not fixed: SledgeHammer (+2.3) and NanoDeath (+1.4). Running keeps them out of point-blank range, not out of their guns'.

---

## The first cut failed its own gate

A rule for weak bots must not fire against strong ones. The first cut did:

- Top-19 mean share fell from **48.6% to 43.6%** (Raven -14.4, Neuromancer -15.2)
- Replaying the detectors over 81 top-19 battles' truth logs showed what separates them: no top-19 robot rammed in more than one round of a battle, while every rammer did in 13 to 35 of 35
- The final rule: ram in two rounds, or 120 mirror scans. Top 19: **48.8%**, detectors off.

---

## 3.5.1: review fixes

Review findings in 3.5's weak-bot responses (R9):

- MIR-1 now aims from where the plan puts Hadur *next* tick, not a straight-line guess
- RAM-2 models a rammer charging while driving backward in its real direction

On the same 11-bot set, the mean rose from **86.7% to 88.3%**. MirrorMicro 83.3% to 91.7%, RedBull 87.4% to 93.7%.

---

## What is next

- **3.5.1 has not been rated live in the archive yet.** The latest saved page is 3.4's.
- The weak tier, SledgeHammer-style nanos, and the 447 opponents at 50 to 70 APS (they cost 6.78 of the 14.1 APS Hadur gives up; the top 19 cost 0.82)
- Warm memory has never shown a measured gain over cold (L-13, issue #49)
- Issue #80 tracks the leak. MEM-14, TIME-7 and RES-11 were left for later.

---

## What it taught us

- Read the PBI by tier: the strong are fine, the next ranks are in the weak
- Prove a leak is reproducible before you fix it
- Gate a rule on behaviour the strong never show, and check it offline first
- A failed first cut is data. Report it, then tighten the rule.

